INT. SLEEP SHACK MATTRESS SHOWROOM - DAY

A beige warehouse of identical beds under cheap track lights. A banner reads "EVERYTHING MUST GO." Soft elevator music plays from a ceiling speaker.

LEN HASHIMOTO, 40s, salesman, short-sleeved dress shirt and a tie with a stain. He's laid out across a king-size mattress, eyes closed, arms folded like a pharaoh.

MILTON VOSS, 60s, the owner, bald, sweater vest, enters from the back office. He stops at the foot of the bed.

MILTON
Len.

LEN
(eyes closed)
I'm testing the product.

MILTON
It's ten in the morning.

LEN
It's a firm that doesn't feel firm. That takes research.

Milton sighs and sits on the next bed. He bounces once. A spring groans.

The store phone RINGS. Milton lifts the cordless handset and holds it away from his ear. A tiny, cheerful voice chirps from the speaker.

PHYLLIS (V.O.)
Is this Sleep Shack? I'd like to return the mattress.

MILTON
Of course, Phyllis. Which one?

PHYLLIS (V.O.)
The one you sold me in 1998.

Milton covers the phone. Len opens one eye.

LEN
She's calling again?

MILTON
She says it's lumpy.

LEN
It's a bed, Milt. It's supposed to be lumpy. It's an emotional lump.

Milton uncovers the phone.

MILTON
Phyllis, we have a lifetime guarantee. Bring it in and we'll look at it.

PHYLLIS (V.O.)
I'm calling from the mattress.

Silence. Len sits up. Milton stares at the handset.

LEN
Where's she calling from?

MILTON
(muted)
She says the mattress.

LEN
Tell her we'll send a truck.

MILTON
(into phone)
We'll send a truck.

PHYLLIS (V.O.)
Please hurry. It's very soft in here.
